# ztypeset -- one line-ending truth, and two trees whose bytes are the contract.
#
# `core.autocrlf` defaults to true on a Windows GitHub runner. Its checkout
# therefore rewrote every LF to CRLF, and the first hosted run failed on that
# alone: `zig fmt --check` listed every Zig source it was given, none of which
# had been touched. A checkout that does not match the commit is not a
# formatting problem, and it cannot be fixed in the step that noticed it.
#
# So: LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every host. This also
# stops a CRLF file entering in the first place, which is the half a checkout
# setting alone does not cover.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two exceptions, and they are not stylistic -- both are trees whose exact
# bytes something checks:
#
#   - libs/ is four pristine upstream trees. ci/verify-vendor.sh proves it by
#     diffing them against a fresh clone of the pinned commit, and normalising
#     line endings on either side would make that diff a function of the host
#     rather than of the tree. Nothing in libs/ is ours to normalise anyway.
#   - tests/fonts/ is verified against SHA256SUMS, and the golden shaping
#     results in the suite are only meaningful against those exact bytes.
#
# These lines come after the rule above because a later pattern overrides an
# earlier one; written the other way round they would do nothing at all.
libs/**        -text
tests/fonts/** -text

# Fonts anywhere: never diffed or merged as text.
*.ttf binary
